⟦2000⟧{{smaller block|<sup>12</sup> Lucian, ''Dial. Meretr.'', ii. 1. On the other hand, we read in Strabo (i. 3, 20) of virgins at Alponus ascending a tower as spectators (κατὰ θέαν) of the Thesmophoria. which would seem to imply that they did not participate in it.}}

⟦2100⟧{{smaller block|<sup>13</sup> Isaeus, ''De Cironis Hered.'', 19; Id., ''De Pyrrhi Hered.'', 80.}}

⟦2200⟧{{smaller block|<sup>14</sup> Aristoph., ''Thesm.'', 624, 658, with the Schol. ''ad ll.'' As to the custom of camping out at festivals, Plutarch (''Quaest. Conviv.'', iv. 6, 2) compares the Jewish Feast of Tabernacles with the Greek Dionysia; from which we may perhaps infer that the worshippers camped out at the Dionysia. Cf. J. Gumilla, ''Histoire de l'Orénoque'', i. p. 256 sq. [1758].}}

⟦2300⟧{{smaller block|<sup>15</sup> Clem. Alex., ''Protrep.'', ch. ii. [p. 16, ed. Potter]; Schol. on Sophocles, ''Oed. Col.'', 681.}}

⟦2400⟧{{smaller block|<sup>16</sup> Marcellinus on Hermogenes, in ''Rhetores Graeci'', ed. Walz, iv. 462; Sopater, ''ibid.'', viii. 67.}}

⟦2500⟧{{smaller block|<sup>17</sup> Aristoph., ''Thesm.'', 80. The word τρίτη seems to mean the Nesteia, as the Schol. ''ad l.'' takes it. That the “middle day” was the Nesteia we know from Athenaeus, vii. 307 f.}}

⟦2600⟧{{smaller block|<sup>18</sup> Xenophon, ''Hellen.'', v. 2, 29; Plutarch, ''Quaest. Gr.'', 31; Athenaeus, xiv. 647''a''.}}

⟦2600⟧{{smaller block|<sup>19</sup> Polyaenus, v. 1, 1; Herodotus, vi. 16; Pausanias, x. 33, 11.}}

⟦2700⟧{{smaller block|<sup>20</sup> C. T. Newton discovered in the sanctuary of Demeter and the Infernal Deities at Cnidus a chamber which may have been one of the ''megara'' referred to by the scholiast. It contained bones of pigs and marble figures of pigs. The chamber was not, however, originally subterranean. See Newton's ''Discoveries at Halicarnassus'' (1863), ii. p. 383, ''Travels and Discoveries in the Levant'' (1865), ii. p. 180 sq. According to Porphyry (''De Antro Nympharum'', 6) the Infernal Deities had ''megara'', as the Olympian had temples, and the sacrificial pits of the former corresponded to the altars of the latter.}}

⟦3000⟧<sup>21</sup> Compare the functions of the two Arrephoroi at Athens (Paus., i. 27, 3). For serpents in connexion with Demeter, compare Strabo ix. 1, 9.
